          "content": "# F4: CoT self-verification & reflection loops\n\n## Findings\n\n### [1] Self-Verification (Weng et al., 2023): forward reasoning + backward verification using Condition Mask Verification improves arithmetic reasoning by 2-4% without finetuning\n- quote: \"Our method employs LLMs for self-verification with only a few prompts, eliminating the need for fine-tuning or gradient updating. This approach enables automatic verification of multiple candidate answers and corresponding conclusions, mitigating deviations from the correct thought chain in the original CoT.\"\n- url: https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.09561\n- source_type: primary (peer-reviewed, EMNLP 2023 Findings)\n- published: 2022-12 (updated 2023)\n- confidence: high\n\n### [2] Self-Verification on Instruct-GPT (code-davinci-002) achieves GSM8K: 60.81→65.14 (+4.33), SingleEq: 91.01→93.40 (+2.39), MultiArith: 96.13→99.15 (+3.02)\n- quote: \"Our method achieved a new state-of-the-art (SOTA) performance in six of these eight datasets. The proposed self-verification method (SV) can improve previous methods in all datasets. self-verification led to an average increase of 2.33% in the high-performing Instruct-GPT model.\"\n- url: https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.09561\n- source_type: primary\n- published: 2022-12\n- confidence: high\n\n### [3] Chain-of-Verification (CoVe, Dhuliawala et al., 2024): four-step verify-then-revise pipeline reduces hallucinations; factored execution (isolating verification from baseline context) is critical to avoid copying errors\n- quote: \"When answering the set of verification questions, we showed that controlling the attention of the model so that it cannot attend to its previous answers (factored CoVe) helps alleviate copying the same hallucinations. Overall, our method provides substantial performance gains over the original language model response just by asking the same model to deliberate on (verify) its answer.\"\n- url: https://aclanthology.org/2024.findings-acl.212/\n- source_type: primary (peer-reviewed, ACL 2024 Findings)\n- published: 2024-08\n- confidence: high\n\n### [4] CoVe factor+revise variant improves FACTSCORE from 63.7 to 71.4 on longform text generation through explicit cross-check of verification answers against baseline response\n- quote: \"In the longform generation task we also explore more sophisticated reasoning steps in the CoVe 'factor+revise' method, which explicitly cross-checks whether verification answers indicate an inconsistency. We see large gains in the FACTSCORE metric from this further explicit reasoning from 63.7 (factored) → 71.4 (factor+revise).\"\n- url: https://aclanthology.org/2024.findings-acl.212.pdf\n- source_type: primary\n- published: 2024-08\n- confidence: high\n\n### [5] Deductive verification of reasoning chains (Ling et al., 2023): decomposing verification into step-by-step subprocesses with minimal premises improves verification accuracy from ~50% to ~69% over whole-chain verification\n- quote: \"We propose to decompose a reasoning verification process into a series of step-by-step subprocesses, each only receiving their necessary context and premises. We found that, if the reasoning process is very short, and only based on necessary premises and contexts, the verification of existing large language models is already quite reliable.\"\n- url: https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.03872\n- source_type: primary\n- published: 2023-06\n- confidence: high\n\n### [6] Self-Refine (Madaan et al., 2023): single LLM iteratively generates, provides feedback, and refines its own output across 7 diverse tasks; improves ~20% absolute over one-shot generation using GPT-3.5/4\n- quote: \"Self-Refine does not require any supervised training data, additional training, or reinforcement learning, and instead uses a single LLM as the generator, refiner, and feedback provider. Across all evaluated tasks, outputs generated with Self-Refine are preferred by humans and automatic metrics over those generated with the same LLM using conventional one-step generation, improving by ~20% absolute on average in task performance.\"\n- url: https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.17651\n- source_type: primary (peer-reviewed, NeurIPS 2023)\n- published: 2023-03\n- confidence: high\n\n### [7] Reflexion (Shinn et al., 2023): verbal reinforcement learning via episodic memory of self-reflections achieves 91% pass@1 on HumanEval, surpassing GPT-4's 80% without weight updates\n- quote: \"Reflexion agents verbally reflect on task feedback signals, then maintain their own reflective text in an episodic memory buffer to induce better decision-making in subsequent trials. Reflexion achieves a 91% pass@1 accuracy on the HumanEval coding benchmark, surpassing the previous state-of-the-art GPT-4 that achieves 80%.\"\n- url: https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.11366\n- source_type: primary (peer-reviewed, NeurIPS 2023)\n- published: 2023-03\n- confidence: high\n\n### [8] General-purpose verifiers (Vacareanu et al., 2024): three constraint-based verifiers (Relevance, Mathematical Accuracy, Logical Consistency) applied at step-level with perplexity scoring consistently outperform best-of-N sampling across 9 reasoning datasets\n- quote: \"We propose three general principles that a model should adhere to while reasoning: (i) Relevance, (ii) Mathematical Accuracy, and (iii) Logical Consistency. We apply these constraints to the reasoning steps generated by the LLM to improve the accuracy of the final generation. The constraints are applied in the form of verifiers: the model itself is asked to verify if the generated steps satisfy each constraint.\"\n- url: https://arxiv.org/pdf/2405.00204\n- source_type: primary\n- published: 2024-04-30\n- confidence: high\n\n### [9] CoT-decoding (Wang & Zhou, 2024): LLMs can reason without any prompting by altering the decoding process — exploring alternative top-k tokens reveals inherent CoT reasoning paths that greedy decoding obscures\n- quote: \"Our findings reveal that, intriguingly, CoT reasoning paths can be elicited from pre-trained LLMs by simply altering the decoding process. Rather than conventional greedy decoding, we investigate the top-alternative tokens, uncovering that CoT paths are frequently inherent in these sequences. This approach not only bypasses the confounders of prompting but also allows us to assess the LLMs' intrinsic reasoning abilities.\"\n- url: https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.10200\n- source_type: primary (peer-reviewed, ICLR 2024)\n- published: 2024-02-15\n- confidence: high\n\n### [10] CoT-decoding with 10 paths achieves GSM8K 63.2% on PaLM-2 L vs 34.8% greedy, and 25.1% on Mistral-7B vs 9.9% greedy — all without any CoT prompt markup\n- quote: \"GSM8K: Mistral-7B greedy decoding 9.9% → CoT-decoding (10 paths) 25.1%; PaLM-2 L greedy decoding 34.8% → CoT-decoding (10 paths) 63.2%\"\n- url: https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.10200\n- source_type: primary\n- published: 2024-02-15\n- confidence: high\n\n### [11] Self-verification is an emergent property of model scale: 175B models reliably self-verify while smaller models (<7B) show negative or no benefit from the same mechanism\n- quote: \"The experimental results suggest that the self-verification ability tends to be more robust as the number of parameters increases... This indicates that self-verification is an emergent property that arises in larger models, where stronger reasoning capacities allow them to derive reliable and accurate conclusions during the context learning process.\"\n- url: https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.09561\n- source_type: primary\n- published: 2022-12\n- confidence: high\n\n### [12] Zero-shot self-verification via COTR-prompt (asking the model to \"double-check step by step\") and COT STEP decomposition enables verification without any few-shot exemplars, though verifier scores do not consistently improve self-consistency aggregation\n- quote: \"Zero-shot verifier scores do not particularly help in augmenting self-consistency. Using zero-shot verifier scores to guide reasoning step search in a step-wise stochastic greedy Beam search did not help either.\"\n- url: https://arxiv.org/pdf/2501.13122\n- source_type: primary\n- published: 2025-01 (approx.)\n- confidence: medium\n\n## Dead ends\n- \"chain-of-thought self-verification LLM reflection revision loop prompt-free control\" → SearXNG returned irrelevant blockchain/crypto results (SearXNG instance had poor coverage for ML topics)\n- \"LLM self-reflection iterative self-critique revision without prompt engineering\" → SearXNG returned only generic LLM overview articles\n- \"Self-Refine LLM iterative self-feedback self-revision Madaan 2023\" → SearXNG returned travel/holiday results\n- \"Reflexion LLM agent self-reflection Shinn 2023 action revision\" → SearXNG returned Office 365 login pages\n- Exa search API hit rate limit before second query could complete\n\n## Suggested follow-ups\n- How well do self-verification and reflection methods transfer to open-source models smaller than 70B (e.g., Llama-3, Mistral) — the emergent-scale finding suggests they may not work below a threshold\n- Empirical comparison of compute cost: how many extra tokens do CoVe/Reflexion/Self-Refine add per query vs. baseline, and at what accuracy gain-per-token tradeoff\n- Can CoT-decoding (decoding-level reasoning extraction) be combined with self-verification for a completely prompt-markup-free control pipeline — both techniques avoid prompt engineering\n"
